LXMF-rs (Reticulum transport and LXMF wire format)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Coyote depends on the `reticulum-rs-transport` and `lxmf-wire` crates from
LXMF-rs (https://github.com/FreeTAKTeam/LXMF-rs). LXMF-rs is dual-licensed:
its package metadata declares `license = "EPL-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-or-later"`, and
its LICENSE file carries the Eclipse Public License 2.0 text followed by a
completed Secondary Licenses Notice offering the source under the "GNU
General Public License, version 2 or any later version".

Coyote relies on the GPL-2.0-or-later arm of that disjunction, which is the
only arm compatible with Coyote's AGPL-3.0-only license (GPL-2.0-or-later
permits GPL-3.0, which AGPL-3.0 section 13 allows to be combined with AGPL
work). The EPL-2.0 arm is not compatible with the GNU General Public License
family and is not relied upon here.

Two obligations of Coyote's own remain outstanding from these dependencies.
The rest are discharged where they are recorded below, or folded into these
two. Neither of the two is an assertion of wrongdoing upstream.

  - License texts the distributed binary relies on are not shipped with it.
    Relying on a GPL arm means shipping a copy of that license, and LXMF-rs
    ships the EPL-2.0 text and the Secondary Licenses Notice but no copy of
    the GNU General Public License; neither does this repository. The same
    is true of the BSD 3-Clause text the dalek crates and `subtle` below are
    under. This one blocks a release.
  - Coyote's package metadata still declares `license = "AGPL-3.0-only"`,
    which no longer describes the combined work once these dependencies
    ship. Settling the aggregate expression is the license owner's call and
    is tracked separately, not as a release gate.

Transitive dependencies. `reticulum-rs-transport` enables its default
`storage` feature, which pulls in `rusqlite` with upstream's `bundled`
setting and therefore compiles the SQLite C amalgamation into Coyote.
`rusqlite` and `libsqlite3-sys` are distributed under the MIT License,
Copyright (c) 2014 The rusqlite developers; the MIT permission notice is
reproduced in the LICENSE-MIT file. That file carries only the Coyote and
AIChat copyright lines; for every third party named here under the MIT
License, the permission text is the shared one in LICENSE-MIT and the
copyright notice is the one given inline below. SQLite itself is in the
public domain.

`reticulum-rs-transport` also pulls in `bzip2`, Copyright (c) 2014 Alex
Crichton, and `bzip2-sys`, Copyright (c) 2014-2025 Alex Crichton and
Contributors, both distributed under `MIT/Apache-2.0` and used here under
the MIT License. `bzip2-sys` ships libbzip2, "copyright (C) 1996-2019 Julian R
Seward", and compiles it on Windows and anywhere pkg-config finds no system
libbz2; where one exists it links that instead. libbzip2's BSD-style license
requires source redistributions to retain its copyright notice and
conditions; that notice travels with the bundled source in the `bzip2-sys`
crate.

`x25519-dalek` arrives with these dependencies and is distributed under the
BSD 3-Clause License:

    Copyright (c) 2017-2021 isis agora lovecruft. All rights reserved.
    Copyright (c) 2019-2021 DebugSteven. All rights reserved.

That license requires binary redistributions to reproduce those notices
together with its list of conditions and its disclaimer. The notices are
recorded here; the license text itself is not shipped, which is the
release-blocking obligation recorded above. The sibling `curve25519-dalek`
and `ed25519-dalek` crates are under the same license with their own
copyright lines; they predate these dependencies and their notices are not
recorded here either, which the same obligation covers. `subtle`, already in
the tree via `digest` and now a direct dependency for the mesh trust store, is
under the same BSD 3-Clause License, "Copyright (c) 2016-2017 Isis Agora
Lovecruft, Henry de Valence. All rights reserved. Copyright (c) 2016-2024 Isis
Agora Lovecruft. All rights reserved."

`reticulum-rs-transport` depends on `tokio-serial` unconditionally, which
brings in `mio-serial` and `serialport`. `tokio-serial` is under the MIT
License, Copyright (c) 2017 Zac Berkowitz and other tokio-serial
contributors; `mio-serial` is under the MIT License, Copyright (c) 2017 Zac
Berkowitz (zac.berkowitz@gmail.com). `serialport` is distributed under the
Mozilla Public License 2.0 and needs no copyright line here. It is used
